<h3>6. Choose Light Colours Throughout</h3>
Dark finishes can make already small kitchens feel more enclosed.

Lighter walls, cabinets and splashbacks reflect natural light and visually expand the room. Pairing these with warm timber accents prevents the space from feeling clinical.

Consistency in colour also helps create a more seamless look, whether updating a central apartment or coordinating family spaces across our <a href="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/stanmore/">Stanmore home renovations</a>.
<h3>7. Keep Your Worktops Clear</h3>
One of the biggest reasons compact kitchens feel cramped is visual clutter.

Store appliances that aren't used daily, organise utensils inside drawers and use attractive storage jars to minimise unnecessary items on display.

A tidy worktop instantly makes the room feel larger.
<h3>8. Upgrade the Splashback</h3>
A new splashback can dramatically refresh the space without requiring a full renovation.

Large-format tiles, textured ceramics or even peel-and-stick splashback panels offer an affordable way to introduce personality while protecting your walls. Prior to committing to finishes, generating <a href="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/3d-render-services/">photorealistic 3D kitchen renders</a> helps you visualise texture, lighting, and contrast accurately.
